WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.534 --> 00:00:03.767
-We're hanging out with the one
and only Drew Barrymore!

00:00:03.767 --> 00:00:05.934
Drew --
Oh, lookit, I love it.

00:00:05.934 --> 00:00:08.133
Come on. The greatest.
Come on. The greatest.

00:00:08.133 --> 00:00:09.434
[ Rimshot ]

00:00:09.434 --> 00:00:10.868
Thank you, thank you, thank you.

00:00:10.868 --> 00:00:14.000
Drew, we're a few months
into the new year,

00:00:14.000 --> 00:00:16.267
and you told me that
your mantra for this year,

00:00:16.267 --> 00:00:21.200
2022, is the Year of Know --
K-N-O-W.

00:00:21.200 --> 00:00:23.334
-That's right, the Year of Know.
-The Year of Know.

00:00:23.334 --> 00:00:25.434
What does this mean?
-Like Carson, Kreskin.

00:00:25.434 --> 00:00:27.234
-Yeah.
Kreskin, yeah.

00:00:27.234 --> 00:00:29.868
-Oh, the best.
-Yeah.

00:00:29.868 --> 00:00:32.601
-Last year was
the Year of Less.

00:00:32.601 --> 00:00:36.868
-You want less of things.
-Yes, I'm very maximalist,

00:00:36.868 --> 00:00:40.200
gluttonous, live in extremes,
big personality --

00:00:40.200 --> 00:00:43.067
nuts, you know, a little bit.
[ Laughter ]

00:00:43.067 --> 00:00:45.567
And -- But a student of life,

00:00:45.567 --> 00:00:47.801
always working on myself,
and every day,

00:00:47.801 --> 00:00:49.534
I get a little bit better,
hopefully.

00:00:49.534 --> 00:00:52.300
-Yeah.
-And so I thought,

00:00:52.300 --> 00:00:55.968
"What if I took this year,
as, you know,

00:00:55.968 --> 00:01:00.901
just trying to be really aware
of everything I'm learning?"

00:01:00.901 --> 00:01:04.968
I feel like we're constantly --
I'm constantly one step forward,

00:01:04.968 --> 00:01:07.934
a step back.
And I just thought

00:01:07.934 --> 00:01:10.601
if I could have more confidence
in what I know.

00:01:10.601 --> 00:01:11.901
And I don't know about you guys,

00:01:11.901 --> 00:01:15.901
but my inner dialogue
is not kind,

00:01:15.901 --> 00:01:20.434
and I would never speak or treat
anyone the way I treat myself.

00:01:20.434 --> 00:01:24.133
And knowing that,
that's the Year of Know --

00:01:24.133 --> 00:01:26.467
knowing things, recognizing it.

00:01:26.467 --> 00:01:28.834
Even if you're
going on a date with someone,

00:01:28.834 --> 00:01:30.934
we all kind of hard launch,
and, like,

00:01:30.934 --> 00:01:32.067
"What is the future
going to bring?"

00:01:32.067 --> 00:01:34.267
Just enjoy things.

00:01:34.267 --> 00:01:37.901
Don't worry about the future
and the past.

00:01:37.901 --> 00:01:39.834
Everyone says,
"Try to be present,

00:01:39.834 --> 00:01:42.234
try to have balance."
I get so irritated

00:01:42.234 --> 00:01:45.767
when they talk like that,
but maybe they're right, so...

00:01:45.767 --> 00:01:47.067
-That that makes you
more irritated.

00:01:47.067 --> 00:01:49.667
-Yes, which is even more
patronizing and annoying.

00:01:49.667 --> 00:01:51.300
-Gosh, yeah.
-So I'm like,

00:01:51.300 --> 00:01:53.334
"Instead of the year
of running around

00:01:53.334 --> 00:01:55.467
like the crazy headless chicken,

00:01:55.467 --> 00:01:57.834
what if I try to act
like this year

00:01:57.834 --> 00:02:00.300
that everything I learn

00:02:00.300 --> 00:02:03.133
is something I can truly keep,
and the knowledge is there,

00:02:03.133 --> 00:02:05.501
and just have
a little more faith in myself?"

00:02:05.501 --> 00:02:07.367
-Love this.
This is fantastic.

00:02:07.367 --> 00:02:08.968
I love that you do that.
I mean, you --

00:02:08.968 --> 00:02:11.133
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:02:11.133 --> 00:02:13.200
The one thing -- another great
thing about the show is,

00:02:13.200 --> 00:02:15.934
you really just put yourself
out there, and a good example,

00:02:15.934 --> 00:02:17.968
I remember you had the cast
of "Queer Eye" come on...

00:02:17.968 --> 00:02:19.334
-Oh, my God.
It changed my life.

00:02:19.334 --> 00:02:21.200
-...and do a makeover for you

00:02:21.200 --> 00:02:23.734
and had you do
an actual live date.

00:02:23.734 --> 00:02:24.934
-Yes.
-And you filmed it,

00:02:24.934 --> 00:02:28.000
and you showed
you going on a live Zoom date.

00:02:28.000 --> 00:02:30.734
-Yeah, which I thought the idea
of a Zoom date was, like,

00:02:30.734 --> 00:02:33.033
hell on Earth.
I don't know why, but --

00:02:33.033 --> 00:02:35.868
I'm so IRL.
You know what I mean?

00:02:35.868 --> 00:02:37.667
-In real life.
Yeah, you're so IRL.

00:02:37.667 --> 00:02:39.534
-I'm so IRL that I was like,
"There's no way..."

00:02:39.534 --> 00:02:42.801
-"I mean, totally be IRL!"
-"...I will like a Zoom date."

00:02:42.801 --> 00:02:45.334
-"I know!
A 'Zuhm dut.'"

00:02:45.334 --> 00:02:48.133
-I -- I -- I -- I tried it.

00:02:48.133 --> 00:02:52.267
It turns out it's not as
prophylactic weird as it seems.

00:02:52.267 --> 00:02:54.267
I guess we're all
sort of, kind of --

00:02:54.267 --> 00:02:56.801
This is the new normal.
The water's out of the bottle.

00:02:56.801 --> 00:02:59.267
It's not going back in.
It's dating apps and Zooms,

00:02:59.267 --> 00:03:03.334
and maybe you can look at it
as a positive of a screener

00:03:03.334 --> 00:03:04.901
or a time-saver.
I don't know.

00:03:04.901 --> 00:03:07.601
I still want to meet someone
the old-fashioned way.

00:03:07.601 --> 00:03:08.701
-Right?
-I want to --

00:03:08.701 --> 00:03:10.234
You know,
I wrote, actually, a paper

00:03:10.234 --> 00:03:12.634
that I'm gonna put on my blog
because I love writing

00:03:12.634 --> 00:03:14.734
and I have a blog
and I've written books,

00:03:14.734 --> 00:03:16.467
and sometimes
you just get inspired.

00:03:16.467 --> 00:03:21.801
And I -- I walked up to a guy
in a park, and I was like,

00:03:21.801 --> 00:03:24.734
"Are you single?"
[ Laughter ]

00:03:24.734 --> 00:03:25.801
And...

00:03:25.801 --> 00:03:28.968
-Did his brain explode
out of his head?

00:03:28.968 --> 00:03:31.067
-And he was like, "Perhaps,"
and I said,

00:03:31.067 --> 00:03:32.834
"Well, that's not 'no.'"
[ Laughter ]

00:03:32.834 --> 00:03:35.367
-So...
-"I'll take 'perhaps.'"

00:03:35.367 --> 00:03:36.767
-And then I was like,
"Are you gay?"

00:03:36.767 --> 00:03:39.467
Because I also
always bark up the wrong tree.

00:03:39.467 --> 00:03:40.534
[ Laughter ]

00:03:40.534 --> 00:03:42.934
And he was like,
"No, I'm not."

00:03:42.934 --> 00:03:46.968
And then we got to talking,
and we had such a good laugh

00:03:46.968 --> 00:03:50.601
and such a lovely talk.
And then I was like,

00:03:50.601 --> 00:03:51.934
"Ooh, I think I forgot
to ask you

00:03:51.934 --> 00:03:54.968
a very important question.
How old are you?"

00:03:54.968 --> 00:03:57.000
-Oh.
-And he was like, "28,"

00:03:57.000 --> 00:03:59.567
and I was like, "Aah! Oh, no!"
[ Laughter ]

00:03:59.567 --> 00:04:01.834
"Oh, no!"
-Oh, come on.

00:04:01.834 --> 00:04:03.601
-And I was like,
"I don't mean this rudely.

00:04:03.601 --> 00:04:06.100
You look so much older."
[ Laughter ]

00:04:06.100 --> 00:04:08.400
And he was like,
"It's funny because it's a joke

00:04:08.400 --> 00:04:10.534
amongst my friends.
They all -- We --

00:04:10.534 --> 00:04:13.067
It's a joke in my life.
I look much older."

00:04:13.067 --> 00:04:15.100
-Yeah.
-And I was like, "You do."

00:04:15.100 --> 00:04:17.567
-Yeah.
"We had a great afternoon.

00:04:17.567 --> 00:04:18.667
Have a great life, bud."

00:04:18.667 --> 00:04:21.400
-And I just said, "Okay.
That was so fun,

00:04:21.400 --> 00:04:24.067
and thank you so much."
We had a great conversation.

00:04:24.067 --> 00:04:26.367
And we didn't bother
with the obligatory, like,

00:04:26.367 --> 00:04:28.767
"Let's exchange numbers,"
and I just said, "Great."

00:04:28.767 --> 00:04:32.300
I said, "I got to tell you,
I just wanted to do something

00:04:32.300 --> 00:04:34.367
that I could make
myself proud today,"

00:04:34.367 --> 00:04:36.133
and he goes, "I'm proud of you."

00:04:36.133 --> 00:04:38.701
[ Applause ]
And I was like, "Thanks."

00:04:38.701 --> 00:04:41.033
-Fantastic.
"We got to hang out more."

00:04:41.033 --> 00:04:46.701
-And we walked away, and it was
the most positive experience.

00:04:46.701 --> 00:04:48.534
I want to do it again.

00:04:48.534 --> 00:04:50.601
Was it dating apps
that stopped us

00:04:50.601 --> 00:04:54.868
from going up to people
and asking them out?

00:04:54.868 --> 00:04:56.868
Now we don't have to worry
about public rejection?

00:04:56.868 --> 00:04:59.200
We can just hide behind the app?
Forget it. I'm over it.

00:04:59.200 --> 00:05:02.400
I want to --
I want to talk to people IRL.

00:05:02.400 --> 00:05:04.501
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:05:04.501 --> 00:05:07.534
-Yes!

00:05:07.534 --> 00:05:09.868
One at a time,
let's go through the audience.

00:05:09.868 --> 00:05:12.934
Who want -- No.
[ Laughter ]

00:05:12.934 --> 00:05:14.000
I wanted to see --

00:05:14.000 --> 00:05:15.300
Speaking of
doing something again,

00:05:15.300 --> 00:05:16.400
you did something on your show.

00:05:16.400 --> 00:05:18.133
You do a thing called
"Drewth or Dare"

00:05:18.133 --> 00:05:20.400
every now and then.
-At the end of every show.

00:05:20.400 --> 00:05:22.968
Segment seven, on our way out.
-"Drewth or Dare."

00:05:22.968 --> 00:05:25.100
-You did one...
-And I'll do anything.

00:05:25.100 --> 00:05:27.367
-...and it made me laugh.
And it was --

00:05:27.367 --> 00:05:31.701
Here was the dare -- try to eat
a lemon wedge and not react.

00:05:31.701 --> 00:05:33.100
-Yeah.
-Now people have been doing this

00:05:33.100 --> 00:05:34.467
online, it's on TikTok.

00:05:34.467 --> 00:05:36.701
It is called the Lemon Challenge
or something.

00:05:36.701 --> 00:05:39.100
Let's do it 'cause I want
to start a movement,

00:05:39.100 --> 00:05:43.033
because I was shocked
to find out

00:05:43.033 --> 00:05:44.200
how much I suck at this.

00:05:44.200 --> 00:05:45.467
-All right,
so we'll just grab a wedge,

00:05:45.467 --> 00:05:46.601
and we'll go for it.
This is the Lemon Challenge.

00:05:46.601 --> 00:05:48.167
-And have you ever done it?
-Never.

00:05:48.167 --> 00:05:49.834
-Okay.
-I'm gonna try to pretend

00:05:49.834 --> 00:05:51.033
I'm fine.
-Okay, great, good.

00:05:51.033 --> 00:05:52.267
-Like, big deal -- it's a lemon.
-Let's do this.

00:05:52.267 --> 00:05:54.267
-Yeah, of course.
Cheers, bud.

00:05:54.267 --> 00:05:55.300
Awesome.
-Cheers!

00:05:55.300 --> 00:05:56.834
-Cheers.
Nice to see ya.

00:05:56.834 --> 00:06:00.901
♪♪

00:06:00.901 --> 00:06:03.067
[ Laughter ]

00:06:03.067 --> 00:06:09.634
♪♪

00:06:09.634 --> 00:06:11.000
[ Laughter ]

00:06:11.000 --> 00:06:16.000
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:06:16.000 --> 00:06:18.067
It's just sour.
It's just so --

00:06:18.067 --> 00:06:19.567
It was good
at the beginning part.

00:06:19.567 --> 00:06:21.534
-And by the way,
these are good lemons.

00:06:21.534 --> 00:06:23.133
This is a quality lemon here.

00:06:23.133 --> 00:06:24.801
-It is a decent lemon
right there.

00:06:24.801 --> 00:06:26.634
Just going for it.
Come on, that's a trooper.

00:06:26.634 --> 00:06:27.767
Drew Barrymore, everybody.
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:06:27.767 --> 00:06:29.601
Can you be cooler?
Can you be cooler?

00:06:29.601 --> 00:06:31.634
There's no cool like you.
-I can't believe nobody's

00:06:31.634 --> 00:06:33.968
worn this sweatshirt
all this time.

00:06:33.968 --> 00:06:35.400
No one rocks it like you.

00:06:35.400 --> 00:06:36.901
I mean, look at the shoes,
everything.

00:06:36.901 --> 00:06:37.801
You know how to do it.

00:06:37.801 --> 00:06:38.701
Come on, bud.
-Pink shoe,

00:06:38.701 --> 00:06:40.033
pink lip.
-The pink lip.

00:06:40.033 --> 00:06:41.267
You know the deal.

00:06:41.267 --> 00:06:42.634
I got to say, you know,

00:06:42.634 --> 00:06:43.834
I watch your show
every single day.

00:06:43.834 --> 00:06:45.601
I love it.
Congrats on that.

00:06:45.601 --> 00:06:49.133
"The Drew Barrymore Show"
is awesome, and I --

00:06:49.133 --> 00:06:51.467
[ Cheers and applause ]
-Thank you.

00:06:51.467 --> 00:06:53.267
-I love it, I love --
There's so many things about it

00:06:53.267 --> 00:06:56.133
I love, but it was your birthday
the other day. Happy belated.

00:06:56.133 --> 00:06:59.400
-Thank you -- 2/22/22.
-Two two two two two two two.

00:06:59.400 --> 00:07:01.801
-It's so crazy.
I --

00:07:01.801 --> 00:07:04.834
I mean, like,
I was born in '75 --

00:07:04.834 --> 00:07:07.868
1975.
-Yeah, of course.

00:07:07.868 --> 00:07:10.834
-And you just don't even think
about that future date.

00:07:10.834 --> 00:07:13.400
Like, it's --
it's almost a surreal thing.

00:07:13.400 --> 00:07:15.934
-Yeah.
-Like the year 2000 seemed nuts.

00:07:15.934 --> 00:07:18.200
Remember, we were all going
to party like it's 1999.

00:07:18.200 --> 00:07:19.767
It just, you know --
-Yeah, Y2K, man.

00:07:19.767 --> 00:07:22.267
-I never thought I'd get here,
and I've been looking so forward

00:07:22.267 --> 00:07:25.067
to that auspicious number
of a day

00:07:25.067 --> 00:07:27.234
that we'll kind of only see
once in our lifetime.

00:07:27.234 --> 00:07:29.067
-But then you actually
were hosting your show

00:07:29.067 --> 00:07:30.701
on your birthday?
-I was!

00:07:30.701 --> 00:07:33.067
Which is so happy because I got
to wake up with the kids,

00:07:33.067 --> 00:07:37.133
and then I got to go to work and
our job, our work environment --

00:07:37.133 --> 00:07:40.434
you guys know it's a family.
It's positive.

00:07:40.434 --> 00:07:43.067
It's like the rest of, you know,
your problems

00:07:43.067 --> 00:07:46.534
kind of dissipate and fade away
when you go to work

00:07:46.534 --> 00:07:49.400
because we're all working
on something so positive

00:07:49.400 --> 00:07:52.701
and multifaceted
and that feed our souls.

00:07:52.701 --> 00:07:55.601
And then I got to go home
after work, and I walked in,

00:07:55.601 --> 00:07:58.133
and there was a blindfold
taped to the door and --

00:07:58.133 --> 00:08:01.467
My daughters had put up there.
-Okay.

00:08:01.467 --> 00:08:03.133
Thank you for clearing that up.
[ Laughter ]

00:08:03.133 --> 00:08:06.033
-And I put it on, and I'm led in
by my daughters,

00:08:06.033 --> 00:08:08.567
and Olive learned to play
"Happy Birthday"

00:08:08.567 --> 00:08:10.467
for me on the piano.
She's in lessons,

00:08:10.467 --> 00:08:13.334
[ Audience awws ]
but she taught herself
by herself.

00:08:13.334 --> 00:08:15.067
-Come on.
-And then Frankie --

00:08:15.067 --> 00:08:17.868
And they got me
a Fudgie the Whale cake.

00:08:17.868 --> 00:08:19.734
-Carvel!
Fudgie the Whale's the best!

00:08:19.734 --> 00:08:21.367
-It's the greatest!
-It's the greatest cake.

00:08:21.367 --> 00:08:23.067
-And they made me
beaded necklaces.

00:08:23.067 --> 00:08:24.901
And then our dog, Lucy --

00:08:24.901 --> 00:08:28.334
And, you know, Gary and Lucy
are, like, twinning.

00:08:28.334 --> 00:08:30.701
And -- And then
Lucy jumped on the cake

00:08:30.701 --> 00:08:31.968
and started eating
the whole thing.

00:08:31.968 --> 00:08:34.067
And dogs cannot have chocolate.
-Of course.

00:08:34.067 --> 00:08:36.534
-So within a matter
of just a minute,

00:08:36.534 --> 00:08:38.601
the whole place looked nuts.
-Chaos.

00:08:38.601 --> 00:08:40.501
-And I was like, "This is
the best birthday ever."

00:08:40.501 --> 00:08:42.167
-Yeah, of course, dude!
That's the best. Why not?

00:08:42.167 --> 00:08:44.400
-That's it.
-I was watching your show

00:08:44.400 --> 00:08:45.567
because I was like,
they were celebrating you

00:08:45.567 --> 00:08:47.400
on your show,
and then you got a surprise

00:08:47.400 --> 00:08:50.100
from The Go-Go's,
and they came on.

00:08:50.100 --> 00:08:52.234
They Zoomed on together,
and they gave you this.

00:08:52.234 --> 00:08:54.467
They made you an honorary Go-Go.

00:08:54.467 --> 00:08:56.734
-I mean, that's me.
-That's Drew.

00:08:56.734 --> 00:08:58.868
They -- They --
-I mean --

00:08:58.868 --> 00:09:01.300
-They made this for me,

00:09:01.300 --> 00:09:03.601
and because I went
and I inducted them

00:09:03.601 --> 00:09:05.467
into the Rock &amp; Roll
Hall of Fame.

00:09:05.467 --> 00:09:06.968
Here's -- I have
a photo of that, by the way.

00:09:06.968 --> 00:09:08.701
This is you doing --
This was genius.

00:09:08.701 --> 00:09:11.334
You did this
as you were intro'ing them.

00:09:11.334 --> 00:09:13.634
You started putting on,
you know, a night mask,

00:09:13.634 --> 00:09:15.601
whatever,
a towel over your head.

00:09:15.601 --> 00:09:18.934
It felt sort of like, "Okay.
These are the women that,

00:09:18.934 --> 00:09:20.234
when they came into my life,

00:09:20.234 --> 00:09:23.167
when I was about 6 years old,
they told me,

00:09:23.167 --> 00:09:25.334
'You can do anything you want.
You can --'"

00:09:25.334 --> 00:09:27.534
I mean, Nan and I made
"Charlie's Angels."

00:09:27.534 --> 00:09:29.667
I don't think I would have dared
make a "Charlie's Angels"

00:09:29.667 --> 00:09:31.133
if it wasn't for The Go-Go's.

00:09:31.133 --> 00:09:32.767
And what I loved is,
they didn't say,

00:09:32.767 --> 00:09:34.534
"You have to pretend
to be a man."

00:09:34.534 --> 00:09:35.968
You get to be
with your girlfriends

00:09:35.968 --> 00:09:38.267
and do everything you dream.

00:09:38.267 --> 00:09:41.734
And it was the best sort of
tone-setter for my entire life.

00:09:41.734 --> 00:09:45.934
So I was like, "How do I pay
these women in the right way?"

00:09:45.934 --> 00:09:47.200
And I just thought,
"I'm gonna go back to

00:09:47.200 --> 00:09:49.734
when I was a little girl,
saw that album cover,

00:09:49.734 --> 00:09:52.667
and it changed my life forever,"
and I put the mask on.

00:09:52.667 --> 00:09:54.534
-It was the best moment of the
Rock &amp; Roll Hall of Fame.

00:09:54.534 --> 00:09:56.534
-Oh, my God! Thank you!
I was so nervous.

00:09:56.534 --> 00:09:58.801
-Questlove was there,
and I got a text from him,

00:09:58.801 --> 00:10:01.067
he said, "Yo, your girl
is crushing it right now."

00:10:01.067 --> 00:10:02.467
-I'm so relieved.
-And I was like, "What?

00:10:02.467 --> 00:10:04.367
What is she doing?" He's like,
"Just wait till you see.

00:10:04.367 --> 00:10:06.200
She's intro'ing The Go-Go's,
and it's brilliant."

00:10:06.200 --> 00:10:07.601
-Oh, my God,
I felt so much pressure

00:10:07.601 --> 00:10:08.934
to get that moment right.

00:10:08.934 --> 00:10:13.434
How do you say to someone
or a group of people,

00:10:13.434 --> 00:10:15.634
"My life changed
because of you"?

00:10:15.634 --> 00:10:17.267
And the whole trajectory,
I know,

00:10:17.267 --> 00:10:21.667
ties back to seeing these girls
that just --

00:10:21.667 --> 00:10:23.601
There was
such a wow factor to me.

00:10:23.601 --> 00:10:25.501
It was like The Go-Go's
and Pippi Longstocking

00:10:25.501 --> 00:10:27.901
are my DNA.
[ Laughter ]

00:10:27.901 --> 00:10:30.000
-Pippi Longstocking, of course.
-Of course.

00:10:30.000 --> 00:10:32.634
Girls who were girls
who can do anything.

00:10:32.634 --> 00:10:34.601
I told you already this,
but I loved your interview

00:10:34.601 --> 00:10:36.467
with Donny Osmond.
I thought it was awesome.

00:10:36.467 --> 00:10:38.467
I love Donny Osmond. I was like,
"Dude, it was great."

00:10:38.467 --> 00:10:40.434
You were just great
and asked some great questions.

00:10:40.434 --> 00:10:42.534
That's the part of your show
that I love the best

00:10:42.534 --> 00:10:44.567
because you ask questions,
they're like...

00:10:44.567 --> 00:10:45.567
A lot of the times,
they're like,

00:10:45.567 --> 00:10:46.701
"That's the best question.

00:10:46.701 --> 00:10:47.901
No one's ever asked me that."

00:10:47.901 --> 00:10:49.100
-Well, thank you.
-And I love that you do that.

00:10:49.100 --> 00:10:51.033
Yeah, I love it.
-I was freaked out

00:10:51.033 --> 00:10:53.300
for your interview
with Dua last night.

00:10:53.300 --> 00:10:54.734
-That was.
-That's my pal.

00:10:54.734 --> 00:11:00.100
-That was a breakdown
of humanity and experience

00:11:00.100 --> 00:11:01.701
and "These are the things
I've learned,

00:11:01.701 --> 00:11:03.133
and this is how I've grown."

00:11:03.133 --> 00:11:05.734
It was so informative.
-Dua Lipeeps.

00:11:05.734 --> 00:11:09.467
-I already am, like,
psychotically obsessed with her,

00:11:09.467 --> 00:11:11.267
and now I'm like,
"Oh, no. How --"

00:11:11.267 --> 00:11:12.601
-She's actually cooler
than you even understand.

00:11:12.601 --> 00:11:13.934
-It hurts.
-It actually hurts.

00:11:13.934 --> 00:11:17.467
-But it was great to hear her
talk about real stuff.

00:11:17.467 --> 00:11:18.734
-Yeah.
-You know?

00:11:18.734 --> 00:11:20.033
That's what we want to do --
we want to have fun

00:11:20.033 --> 00:11:21.334
and lean into comedy,

00:11:21.334 --> 00:11:23.300
but I need to get real
every once in a while

00:11:23.300 --> 00:11:24.767
because it's not all...

00:11:24.767 --> 00:11:26.634
-That's why people love you.
-Oh, God.

00:11:26.634 --> 00:11:29.901
-Yeah, because, no, it's good.
You get -- You get emotional.

00:11:29.901 --> 00:11:31.901
You're not afraid
to do any of that stuff.

00:11:31.901 --> 00:11:34.701
-I -- I know our tone
of the show is weird,

00:11:34.701 --> 00:11:37.400
but I'm weird,
and I -- I like comedy.

00:11:37.400 --> 00:11:39.200
It's medicine to me.
I like news.

00:11:39.200 --> 00:11:44.701
I like, you know, food
and design and human interest

00:11:44.701 --> 00:11:46.834
and conversations
and seeing somebody

00:11:46.834 --> 00:11:48.934
you've loved your whole life
walk out and be like,

00:11:48.934 --> 00:11:51.734
"Oh, my God," and someone
you had never met before,

00:11:51.734 --> 00:11:54.534
but you will always remember
them after hearing their story.

00:11:54.534 --> 00:11:55.834
Everybody has a story.

00:11:55.834 --> 00:11:56.801
-Everyone's got a story.

